(window.CE_PACK=window.CE_PACK||{})["Parallel & Distributed Computing"]=Object.assign((window.CE_PACK||{})["Parallel & Distributed Computing"]||{},{docs:{"assign":"# Parallel and Distributed Computing: Assignments with Worked Solutions\n\nShow formulas and substitute numbers. All numerical answers below were computed in Python (speedup laws, work-span bounds, clocks, hashing, quorum and queueing calculations); the loop-scheduling loads and the data-race behaviour were also checked by running C/OpenMP/pthreads code. Programs marked \"sketch\" are for reasoning and were not executed.\n\n# Assignment 1: Performance Models and Scalability (70 marks)\n\n## Questions\n\n**Q1. Amdahl's law (12 marks)** A program is 80 percent parallelizable. (a) Compute the speedup and efficiency on 8 and 32 processors and the limit [5]. (b) What parallel fraction is needed for a speedup of 6 on 8 processors? [3] (c) Explain why adding processors beyond a point is wasteful and what to optimize instead [4].\n\n**Q2. Karp-Flatt diagnosis (10 marks)** Measured speedups are 1.9 on 2 processors, 3.4 on 4 and 5.6 on 8. (a) Compute the experimentally determined serial fraction e for each [6]. (b) Is e roughly constant? What does the trend tell you? [4]\n\n**Q3. Work and span (12 marks)** A parallel computation has work T₁ = 4,800 and span T∞ = 60 (unit operations). (a) Compute its parallelism [2]. (b) Give the lower and upper bounds on the time on 8, 32 and 100 processors from Brent's theorem [6]. (c) How many processors are useful? [2] (d) Give work and span for a parallel reduction of 4,096 numbers [2].\n\n**Q4. Roofline (10 marks)** A node has 500 GFLOP/s peak and 100 GB/s memory bandwidth. (a) Compute the ridge point [2]. (b) Find the attainable performance for kernels with arithmetic intensity 0.25, 2 and 10 FLOP/byte and classify each [6]. (c) Name two optimizations for a memory-bound kernel [2].\n\n**Q5. Isoefficiency (12 marks)** Summing n numbers on p processors takes T_p = n/p + 2 log₂ p. (a) Compute speedup and efficiency for (n, p) = (2¹⁶, 64), (2¹⁶, 1024) and (2²⁴, 1024) [6]. (b) How large must n be to keep efficiency at 0.9 with p = 1024? [4] (c) What does it mean for an algorithm to be scalable? [2]\n\n**Q6. Communication cost (6 marks)** With latency α = 2 µs and β = 0.5 ns/byte: (a) Time to send 100 B and 10 MB [2]. (b) The message size at which latency and bandwidth terms are equal [2]. (c) Broadcast of 1 MB to 128 processes with a linear scheme versus a binomial tree [2].\n\n**Q7. Interpreting a scaling study (8 marks)** Run times: p = 1: 120 s; 2: 64 s; 4: 36 s; 8: 23 s; 16: 18 s. Compute the speedup, efficiency and Karp-Flatt value for each p and comment [8].\n\n## Solutions\n\n**Q1.**\n(a) Step 1: S(p) = 1/(0.2 + 0.8/p).\nStep 2: S(8) = 1/(0.2 + 0.1) = **3.33**, efficiency 0.417.\nStep 3: S(32) = 1/(0.2 + 0.025) = **4.44**, efficiency 0.139.\nStep 4: Limit = 1/0.2 = **5**.\n(b) 6 = 1/((1 - f) + f/8) gives (1 - f) + f/8 = 1/6, 1 - 7f/8 = 0.1667, f = 0.8333 × 8/7 = **0.952**.\n(c) Past a few tens of processors the serial 20 percent dominates, so each added processor contributes almost nothing while cost and energy grow. Reduce the serial fraction first: parallelize remaining sections, overlap I/O, remove global synchronization, or use a different algorithm. (Also scale the problem with the machine: Gustafson.)\n\n**Q2.**\n(a) e = (1/ψ - 1/p)/(1 - 1/p). p = 2: (0.5263 - 0.5)/0.5 = **0.053**. p = 4: (0.2941 - 0.25)/0.75 = **0.059**. p = 8: (0.1786 - 0.125)/0.875 = **0.061**.\n(b) The values are small and nearly constant (0.053 to 0.061), rising only slightly: the limit is mostly an inherent serial part of about 5 to 6 percent, with little additional parallel overhead. A strongly increasing e would indicate growing communication or load imbalance.\n\n**Q3.**\n(a) Parallelism = 4,800/60 = **80**.\n(b) Lower bound max(T₁/p, T∞); upper bound T₁/p + T∞. p = 8: 600 to 660. p = 32: 150 to 210. p = 100: max(48, 60) = 60 to 108.\n(c) Up to about 80 processors are useful; beyond that the span (60) limits the time.\n(d) Work = 4,095 additions; span = log₂ 4096 = **12**.\n\n**Q4.**\n(a) Ridge = 500/100 = **5 FLOP/byte**.\n(b) Attainable = min(500, 100 × AI): AI = 0.25 → **25 GFLOP/s** (memory bound); AI = 2 → **200 GFLOP/s** (memory bound); AI = 10 → **500 GFLOP/s** (compute bound).\n(c) Raise arithmetic intensity: cache blocking/tiling, loop fusion, reuse data in registers or shared memory, use lower precision or compression; improve streaming (prefetch, contiguous access).\n\n**Q5.**\n(a) (2¹⁶, 64): T = 1024 + 12 = 1036; S = 65,536/1036 = **63.3**, E = **0.988**. (2¹⁶, 1024): T = 64 + 20 = 84; S = **780**, E = **0.762**. (2²⁴, 1024): T = 16,384 + 20 = 16,404; S = **1022.8**, E = **0.999**.\n(b) E = n/(n + 2p log₂ p) = 0.9 → n = 9 × 2p log₂ p = 9 × 2 × 1024 × 10 = **184,320**.\n(c) An algorithm is scalable if efficiency can be maintained as p grows by increasing the problem size at a reasonable rate (a small isoefficiency function); here n must grow like p log p.\n\n**Q6.**\n(a) 100 B: 2 µs + 0.05 µs = **2.05 µs** (latency dominated). 10 MB: 2 µs + 5 ms = **5.002 ms** (bandwidth dominated).\n(b) α = βm → m = 2×10⁻⁶/0.5×10⁻⁹ = **4,000 bytes**.\n(c) Linear: 127 × (2 µs + 0.5 ms) = **63.8 ms**. Binomial tree: log₂ 128 = 7 steps × 0.502 ms = **3.51 ms** (18 times faster).\n\n**Q7.**\nSpeedup = 120/t: p = 2: 1.88 (E 0.94, e 0.067); p = 4: 3.33 (E 0.83, e 0.067); p = 8: 5.22 (E 0.65, e 0.076); p = 16: 6.67 (E 0.42, e 0.093). The serial fraction estimate starts near 6.7 percent and grows at 8 and 16 processors, so besides an inherent serial part there is **growing parallel overhead** (communication, imbalance or memory contention). Going from 8 to 16 processors saves only 5 s (23 → 18 s) for twice the hardware.\n\n# Assignment 2: Shared-Memory Programming and GPUs (70 marks)\n\n## Questions\n\n**Q1. Races (10 marks)** Two threads each execute `x = x + 1` on a shared integer initially 0, where each statement compiles to load, add, store. (a) List the possible final values and give an interleaving for each [5]. (b) Name three ways to make the program correct [3]. (c) Explain why this program is not fixed by declaring x `volatile` [2].\n\n**Q2. Deadlock (12 marks)** Thread 1 locks A then B; thread 2 locks B then A. (a) Show an interleaving that deadlocks [3]. (b) Name the four Coffman conditions and which one you break with a global lock order [5]. (c) Rewrite the thread bodies to avoid deadlock [2]. (d) What is the difference between deadlock and livelock? [2]\n\n**Q3. Bounded buffer (12 marks)** Write (sketch) a producer-consumer solution with a buffer of N slots using semaphores `empty` (initially N), `full` (initially 0) and a mutex. Explain why the order of the `wait` operations matters, and what happens if the mutex is acquired before the semaphore wait [12].\n\n**Q4. OpenMP (14 marks)** (a) Find the bug in\n```\ndouble sum = 0;\n#pragma omp parallel for\nfor (int i = 0; i < n; i++) sum += a[i];\n```\nand give two fixes [4]. (b) A loop has iteration i costing i units, i = 0..99, run on 4 threads. Compute the load per thread and the imbalance (max/mean) for static block scheduling and for cyclic (chunk 1) scheduling [6]. (c) With dynamic scheduling and chunks of 5 and 10, the greedy schedule finishes with loads [1050, 1175, 1300, 1425] (chunk 5). What is its imbalance and why can bigger chunks be worse? [4]\n\n**Q5. Cache effects (8 marks)** (a) A cache line is 64 bytes. How many 8-byte counters share one line? [2] (b) Four threads increment their own counters stored in `long c[4]`. Explain the performance problem and give a fix; a measured run took 2.0 s for the adjacent layout and 0.9 s when padded [4]. (c) What speedup did the fix give? [2]\n\n**Q6. Contention bound (6 marks)** Each loop iteration does 10 µs of independent work and 2 µs inside a critical section protected by one lock. What is the maximum speedup regardless of the number of threads? Suggest one improvement.\n\n**Q7. GPU sizing (8 marks)** A kernel processes n = 5,000,000 elements with 128-thread blocks. (a) How many blocks are needed? How many warps per block? [3] (b) An SM has 65,536 registers and 2,048 thread slots. With 32 registers per thread how many blocks fit and what is the occupancy? With 48 registers per thread? [5]\n\n## Solutions\n\n**Q1.**\n(a) Step 1: Final values **2** or **1**.\nStep 2: Value 2: thread 1 runs load, add, store completely (x = 1), then thread 2 loads 1, adds, stores (x = 2).\nStep 3: Value 1: both load 0, both add to get 1, both store 1 (a lost update) (Real runs: four threads incrementing 10⁶ times each lost about 0.4 to 0.5 percent of the 4×10⁶ updates in our experiment.)\n(b) Use a mutex around the increment, use an atomic fetch-and-add, or give each thread a private counter and combine after joining (reduction).\n(c) `volatile` only prevents the compiler from caching the value in a register; it does not make the load-add-store sequence atomic and gives no ordering guarantees between threads.\n\n**Q2.**\n(a) T1 locks A; T2 locks B; T1 tries to lock B (blocked, held by T2); T2 tries to lock A (blocked, held by T1). Neither can proceed.\n(b) Mutual exclusion; hold and wait; no preemption; circular wait. A global lock ordering breaks **circular wait**.\n(c) Both threads lock A first and then B (and release in reverse order): `lock(A); lock(B); ...; unlock(B); unlock(A)`.\n(d) In deadlock threads are blocked forever waiting for each other; in livelock threads keep running and changing state in response to each other (for example repeatedly backing off and retrying at the same time) without making progress.\n\n**Q3.** Sketch:\n```\nsemaphore empty = N, full = 0;  mutex m;\nproducer: loop { item = produce(); wait(empty); lock(m); put(item); unlock(m); signal(full); }\nconsumer: loop { wait(full); lock(m); item = get(); unlock(m); signal(empty); consume(item); }\n```\n`empty` counts free slots and blocks producers when the buffer is full; `full` counts items and blocks consumers when empty; the mutex protects the buffer indices from simultaneous modification. The semaphore waits must come **before** taking the mutex: if a producer locked the mutex first and then waited on `empty` with a full buffer, it would hold the mutex while sleeping and the consumer, which needs the mutex to remove an item, could never run: **deadlock**.\n\n**Q4.**\n(a) `sum` is shared and updated by all threads without synchronization: a data race gives wrong, varying results. Fixes: `reduction(+:sum)` (each thread accumulates privately and the partial sums are combined) or `#pragma omp atomic` / `critical` around the update (correct but slow because of serialization).\n(b) Step 1: Total work = 0 + 1 + ... + 99 = 4,950; mean per thread 1,237.5.\nStep 2: **Static block** (25 consecutive iterations each): loads 300, 925, 1550, 2175; imbalance = 2175/1237.5 = **1.76**.\nStep 3: **Cyclic** (thread t takes i ≡ t mod 4): loads 1200, 1225, 1250, 1275; imbalance **1.03**.\nStep 4: The block schedule leaves three threads idle while thread 3 finishes.\n(c) Imbalance = 1425/1237.5 = **1.15** for chunk 5 (the slowest thread sets the time). Chunk 10 ends with loads [890, 1090, 1335, 1635]: imbalance 1.32. Large chunks near the end of the loop leave a big chunk for one thread, while very small chunks add scheduling overhead; guided scheduling (shrinking chunks) balances both.\n\n**Q5.**\n(a) 64/8 = **8** counters per line.\n(b) The four counters lie in one cache line; every write invalidates the line in the other cores' caches (**false sharing**), so the line bounces between cores. Fix: pad each counter to its own line (for example a struct with 56 bytes of padding or `alignas(64)`), or accumulate in a local variable and write once at the end.\n(c) 2.0/0.9 = **2.2×** faster.\n\n**Q6.** The critical section serializes 2 of every 12 µs of work, so the throughput is limited to one iteration per 2 µs: maximum speedup = 12/2 = **6** (Amdahl with a 1/6 serial fraction). Improvements: shrink the critical section, use atomics or per-thread accumulators merged at the end, or shard the lock (lock striping).\n\n**Q7.**\n(a) ⌈5,000,000/128⌉ = **39,063 blocks**; 128/32 = **4 warps** per block.\n(b) 32 registers: register limit 65,536/(32 × 128) = 16 blocks; thread-slot limit 2,048/128 = 16 blocks; so **16 blocks, occupancy 100 percent**. 48 registers: 65,536/(48 × 128) = 10.67 → **10 blocks**, resident threads 1,280, occupancy **62.5 percent**.\n\n# Assignment 3: Distributed Systems (70 marks)\n\n## Questions\n\n**Q1. Logical clocks (14 marks)** Events: P1: a; P2: b; P1: send m1 (to P2); P2: receive m1; P3: c; P2: send m2 (to P3); P3: receive m2; P3: send m3 (to P1); P1: receive m3; P1: d. (a) Assign Lamport timestamps [5]. (b) Assign vector clocks [5]. (c) Using the vector clocks decide whether b and c are concurrent, and whether a happened before d [4].\n\n**Q2. Quorums and availability (12 marks)** (a) For N = 5, give two (R, W) pairs that guarantee that reads see the latest write and one that does not [4]. (b) Each node is up with probability 0.95. Compute the probability that a write quorum is available for N = 5, W = 3; N = 3, W = 2; N = 3, W = 3; N = 5, W = 5 [6]. (c) Which choice would you use for a read-heavy workload and why? [2]\n\n**Q3. Consistent hashing (10 marks)** A ring has positions 0 to 99. Nodes A at 10, B at 40, C at 70. A key belongs to the first node at a position greater than or equal to its hash going clockwise (wrapping around). (a) Assign keys with hashes 5, 15, 45, 75, 95 [3]. (b) Add node D at 55; which keys move and from where to where? [3] (c) Compare with `hash mod N` when N changes from 3 to 4 for keys 0..99, given that 73 of 100 keys change nodes [4].\n\n**Q4. Replication and consensus (12 marks)** (a) A Raft leader of a 5-server cluster has log length 7; the highest replicated indices are 7 (leader), 7, 5, 4, 3. What is the highest index that can be committed? [3] (b) Repeat for a 7-server cluster with indices 9, 8, 8, 6, 2, 1, 1 [3] (c) Why does Raft randomize election timeouts? [3] (d) How many Byzantine faults can a 10-replica system tolerate? [3]\n\n**Q5. Atomic commit and sagas (8 marks)** (a) Count the messages in a successful two-phase commit with 5 participants [3] and in an abort when one participant votes no [3]. (b) What is the blocking problem of 2PC? [2]\n\n**Q6. MapReduce (6 marks)** Three input lines: \"a b a c a\", \"b b a\", \"c c c a b\". (a) How many key-value pairs does the map phase emit without and with a combiner? [3] (b) Give the word counts [3].\n\n**Q7. Latency and tail behavior (10 marks)** A server has service rate 100 requests/s. (a) For arrival rates 50, 80 and 95 requests/s compute utilization, mean response time and mean number in system (M/M/1) [6]. (b) A request fans out to 50 back-end servers, each slow with probability 2 percent; what fraction of requests is slow? [2] (c) Suggest two mitigations [2].\n\n**Q8. CAP classification (8 marks)** Classify a bank's account-balance service and a social network's \"likes\" counter as CP or AP in a partition and justify.\n\n## Solutions\n\n**Q1.**\n(a) Step 1: Lamport: P1: a = 1, send m1 = 2, receive m3 = max(2, 6) + 1 = 7, d = 8.\nStep 2: P2: b = 1, receive m1 = max(1, 2) + 1 = 3, send m2 = 4.\nStep 3: P3: c = 1, receive m2 = max(1, 4) + 1 = 5, send m3 = 6.\n(b) Vector clocks [P1, P2, P3]: a [1,0,0]; b [0,1,0]; send m1 [2,0,0]; receive m1 at P2 [2,2,0]; c [0,0,1]; send m2 [2,3,0]; receive m2 at P3 [2,3,2]; send m3 [2,3,3]; receive m3 at P1 [3,3,3]; d [4,3,3].\n(c) b = [0,1,0] and c = [0,0,1] are incomparable (neither is ≤ the other), so b and c are **concurrent**. a = [1,0,0] ≤ d = [4,3,3] componentwise and different, so **a happened before d**. (The Lamport stamps 1 and 1 for b and c could not show this.)\n\n**Q2.**\n(a) R + W > N guarantees overlap: (R, W) = (3, 3), (2, 4), (1, 5) guarantee latest reads. (2, 2) with N = 5 has R + W = 4 ≤ 5, so a read quorum may miss the write quorum: stale reads possible.\n(b) P(at least k of N nodes up) with p = 0.95: N = 5, W = 3: **0.99884**; N = 3, W = 2: **0.99275**; N = 3, W = 3: **0.85737** (= 0.95³); N = 5, W = 5: **0.77378** (= 0.95⁵). Requiring all replicas for writes sharply reduces write availability.\n(c) A small R and large W, e.g. N = 3, R = 1, W = 3 (fast reads) but writes need every replica; a balanced choice such as N = 5, R = 2, W = 4 or majority quorums (3, 3) keeps both operations tolerant of failures. For read-heavy loads with rare writes, R = 1, W = N is acceptable only if write unavailability under a single failure is tolerable; majority quorums are the safer default.\n\n**Q3.**\n(a) Key 5 → A (10); 15 → B (40); 45 → C (70); 75 → no node at or above 75, wrap to A (10); 95 → wrap to A. Result: {5: A, 15: B, 45: C, 75: A, 95: A}.\n(b) D at 55 takes keys in (40, 55]: only **key 45 moves, from C to D**. Others unchanged.\n(c) With `mod`, 73 of 100 keys move when N goes from 3 to 4, whereas consistent hashing moves only the keys in the new node's arc (about 1/N of the keys on average, here 1 of 5).\n\n**Q4.**\n(a) A log entry is committed when stored on a majority (3 of 5). Sort the indices: 7, 7, 5, 4, 3; the third-highest is **5**: servers with index ≥ 5 are three, so index 5 can be committed (Raft also requires the entry to be from the leader's current term).\n(b) Majority of 7 is 4; sorted 9, 8, 8, 6, 2, 1, 1; the fourth-highest is **6**.\n(c) If all followers timed out simultaneously they would all become candidates and split the votes forever; randomized timeouts (for example 150 to 300 ms) make it likely that one server times out first, wins a majority and sends heartbeats before the others time out.\n(d) n ≥ 3f + 1 gives f ≤ (10 - 1)/3 = **3** Byzantine faults.\n\n**Q5.**\n(a) Success: 5 prepare + 5 vote-yes + 5 commit + 5 ack = **20 messages**. Abort when one votes no: 5 prepare + 5 votes, then abort to the 4 who voted yes + 4 acks = **18 messages**.\n(b) If the coordinator crashes after participants have voted yes and before sending the decision, participants hold locks and cannot decide on their own (they cannot know whether others voted no): the protocol **blocks** until the coordinator recovers. Mitigations: replicated coordinators (Paxos Commit), timeouts with termination protocols, or sagas with compensating transactions.\n\n**Q6.**\n(a) Without combiner: 5 + 3 + 5 = **13 pairs**. With a per-line combiner that sums within a mapper: line 1 emits a, b, c (3 pairs), line 2 emits b, a (2), line 3 emits c, a, b (3): **8 pairs**, a 38 percent reduction in shuffle traffic.\n(b) a: 3 + 1 + 1 = **5**; b: 1 + 2 + 1 = **4**; c: 1 + 0 + 3 = **4**.\n\n**Q7.**\n(a) ρ = λ/μ, W = 1/(μ - λ), L = λ/(μ - λ). λ = 50: ρ = 0.5, W = **20 ms**, L = **1**. λ = 80: ρ = 0.8, W = **50 ms**, L = **4**. λ = 95: ρ = 0.95, W = **200 ms**, L = **19**. Latency explodes as ρ approaches 1.\n(b) 1 - 0.98⁵⁰ = **0.636**: nearly two thirds of requests see at least one slow server.\n(c) Hedged requests (send a second copy after a short delay), reduce fan-out, replicate data and take the fastest answer, use timeouts with partial results, keep utilization below about 70 percent, and isolate slow nodes.\n\n**Q8.** Bank balance: **CP**: correctness matters more than availability; during a partition, refuse or delay operations that cannot reach a quorum rather than risk overdrafts or inconsistent balances. Likes counter: **AP**: the counter can be slightly stale or temporarily diverge; accept updates on both sides and merge afterward (a CRDT counter), prioritizing availability and low latency.\n","exam":"# Parallel and Distributed Computing: Final Examination\n\nDuration: 3 hours. Calculator and a one-page formula sheet allowed. Total: 100 marks.\n- Section A: 20 multiple-choice questions, 1 mark each (20 marks). Answer all.\n- Section B: 8 short questions, 5 marks each (40 marks). Answer all.\n- Section C: 5 long questions, 10 marks each. Answer any four (40 marks).\n\nFormulas: S = T(1)/T(p), E = S/p; Amdahl S = 1/((1 - f) + f/p); Karp-Flatt e = (1/S - 1/p)/(1 - 1/p); Brent max(T₁/p, T∞) ≤ T_p ≤ T₁/p + T∞; message time α + βm; Little's law L = λW; M/M/1 W = 1/(μ - λ); Bloom false-positive rate (1 - e^(-kn/m))^k with k = (m/n) ln 2.\n\n## Section A: Multiple Choice (20 marks)\n\n**1.** Strong scaling measures\nA) speedup when the problem grows with processors  B) speedup of a fixed-size problem as processors increase  C) clock frequency  D) memory size\n\n**2.** Superlinear speedup can occur because\nA) the serial fraction is negative  B) it cannot occur  C) Amdahl's law is wrong  D) more caches in total can make the data fit, reducing memory accesses\n\n**3.** The span of a computation DAG is\nA) the number of threads  B) the length of the longest chain of dependent operations  C) the total number of operations  D) the memory footprint\n\n**4.** A kernel with arithmetic intensity below the roofline ridge point is\nA) compute bound  B) faster on more cores always  C) network bound  D) memory bound\n\n**5.** An atomic fetch-and-add on a shared counter\nA) needs a barrier  B) completes as one indivisible operation  C) disables caches  D) is slower than no synchronization and always loses updates\n\n**6.** A condition variable should be waited on inside a loop because\nA) it saves memory  B) wake-ups may be spurious or the condition may change before the thread runs  C) the loop speeds up the wait  D) the loop is required by the CPU\n\n**7.** The ABA problem concerns\nA) clock drift  B) a compare-and-swap succeeding although the value changed from A to B and back to A  C) cache coloring  D) MPI collectives\n\n**8.** In OpenMP, schedule(dynamic, 8) is best suited to loops whose iterations\nA) contain barriers  B) are empty  C) have irregular, unpredictable costs  D) are all identical\n\n**9.** In a halo exchange for a grid code, the amount of communication per process grows with the\nA) volume of the subdomain  B) number of time steps only  C) program size  D) boundary (surface) of the subdomain\n\n**10.** Two ranks both call blocking MPI_Send to each other before any receive; with large messages this may\nA) double the bandwidth  B) deadlock  C) always succeed  D) trigger a barrier\n\n**11.** In CUDA, divergence within a warp means\nA) memory is freed  B) the kernel crashes  C) warps are split across SMs  D) the warp executes both branch paths serially, reducing efficiency\n\n**12.** Occupancy is limited by\nA) the clock only  B) the compiler's name  C) registers per thread, shared memory per block and threads per SM  D) the disk size\n\n**13.** FLP impossibility states that in a fully asynchronous system\nA) all clocks agree  B) consensus is impossible to guarantee with even one crash failure  C) consensus needs 3f + 1 nodes  D) messages are never lost\n\n**14.** Byzantine fault tolerance of f faulty replicas requires at least\nA) f² replicas  B) f + 1 replicas  C) 2f replicas  D) 3f + 1 replicas\n\n**15.** Two-phase commit blocks when\nA) the coordinator fails after participants have voted yes and before the decision is delivered  B) all vote no  C) a participant votes yes  D) the network is fast\n\n**16.** With hash(key) mod N partitioning, changing N moves\nA) no keys  B) only one key  C) a fixed 1/N of keys  D) almost all keys\n\n**17.** A Bloom filter can produce\nA) neither  B) both false positives and false negatives  C) false negatives only  D) false positives but no false negatives\n\n**18.** Delivery semantics that require idempotent handlers are\nA) at-most-once  B) never  C) exactly-once without retries  D) at-least-once\n\n**19.** According to the tail-at-scale argument, a request that fans out to many servers\nA) is as fast as the fastest server  B) is as slow as its slowest response, so rare slow responses become common  C) sees the average latency  D) is unaffected by slow nodes\n\n**20.** Eventual consistency means that\nA) reads are always the latest  B) if no new updates occur, all replicas eventually converge to the same value  C) replicas never diverge  D) writes are never lost\n\n## Section B: Short Questions (40 marks)\n\n**B1. Amdahl (5 marks)** A program is 95 percent parallelizable. Compute S(8) and S(64), the efficiency at 64, and the limit.\n\n**B2. Karp-Flatt (5 marks)** A speedup of 10 is measured on 16 processors. Compute e and say what a small, constant e indicates.\n\n**B3. Work-span (5 marks)** A computation has work 2,400 and span 30. Give its parallelism and the bounds on the time on 16 processors.\n\n**B4. Domain decomposition (5 marks)** A 4096 × 4096 grid is split over 16 processes either into 1D slabs or 4 × 4 blocks. Compute the cells per process, the halo cells exchanged per step per process in each decomposition (one-cell-wide halo, interior process), and comment.\n\n**B5. MPI deadlock (5 marks)** Two ranks each execute MPI_Send(buf, large, other) followed by MPI_Recv(...). Explain the problem and give two fixes.\n\n**B6. Vector clocks (5 marks)** Compare [2,3,1] with [2,4,1], and [3,1,0] with [2,2,0]: which are ordered and which concurrent?\n\n**B7. Quorums (5 marks)** N = 7 replicas with R = 4 and W = 4. Does the configuration give read-your-writes? How many replica failures can reads and writes each tolerate?\n\n**B8. Bloom filter (5 marks)** A Bloom filter has m = 8,000 bits and n = 500 items. Find the optimal k and the false-positive rate; and the false-positive rate for m = 1,000, n = 100, k = 3.\n\n## Section C: Long Questions (answer any four, 40 marks)\n\n**C1. Performance analysis (10 marks)** Run times of a solver: p = 1: 200 s; 2: 104 s; 4: 58 s; 8: 36 s. (a) Compute speedup, efficiency and Karp-Flatt e for each p [5]. (b) Interpret the e values and suggest causes and remedies [3]. (c) Estimate the speedup on 16 processors if e stays at 0.063 [2].\n\n**C2. Loop scheduling and false sharing (10 marks)** A loop has 200 iterations, iteration i costing i units, run on 4 threads. (a) Compute thread loads and speedup (ideal time = total/4) for static block scheduling and for cyclic (chunk 1) [5]. (b) A parallel histogram uses `long hist[4]` where thread t increments hist[t]; explain the performance problem and give two fixes [3]. (c) State the OpenMP clause that removes the race in a parallel sum [2].\n\n**C3. Event ordering (10 marks)** Events: P1: a; P1: send m1 (to P2); P2: receive m1; P2: send m2 (to P3); P3: c; P3: receive m2; P1: b; P3: send m3 (to P1); P1: receive m3. (a) Lamport timestamps [4]. (b) Vector clocks [4]. (c) Is b concurrent with c? Is a before the receive of m3 at P1? [2]\n\n**C4. Replication and consensus (10 marks)** (a) Nodes are up with probability 0.9. Compute the probability that a majority of 5 nodes is up [2]. (b) A Raft leader in a 5-node cluster has match indices 12, 12, 10, 9, 4 (including itself). What can be committed? [2] (c) Explain how Raft elects a leader and why a majority is needed [3] (d) Describe the CAP choice for a system storing financial ledger entries versus a product-recommendation cache [3].\n\n**C5. Design of a distributed key-value store (10 marks)** Requirements: 10⁹ keys of 1 KB, replication factor 3, 100,000 requests/s of which 20 percent are writes, each node holds up to 1 TB and should be at most 70 percent full. (a) Compute the total storage and the minimum number of nodes [3]. (b) With quorum R = W = 2 compute the node operations per second and the load per node for 6 nodes (assume writes go to 3 replicas and reads query 2) [3]. (c) Explain the partitioning scheme, including virtual nodes, and how failures and node additions are handled [4].\n\n## Answer key and mark scheme\n\n### Section A\n1. B 2. D 3. B 4. D 5. B 6. B 7. B 8. C 9. D 10. B 11. D 12. C 13. B 14. D 15. A 16. D 17. D 18. D 19. B 20. B\n\n### Section B\n\n**B1.** S(8) = 1/(0.05 + 0.95/8) = 1/0.16875 = **5.93**; S(64) = 1/(0.05 + 0.01484) = **15.4**; efficiency at 64 = 15.4/64 = **0.24**; limit = 1/0.05 = **20** [5].\n\n**B2.** e = (1/10 - 1/16)/(1 - 1/16) = (0.1 - 0.0625)/0.9375 = **0.040** [3]. A small constant e means that the speedup limit comes from a small inherent serial fraction (about 4 percent) rather than growing parallel overhead [2].\n\n**B3.** Parallelism = 2,400/30 = **80** [2]. On 16 processors: lower bound max(2400/16, 30) = **150**; upper bound 150 + 30 = **180** [3].\n\n**B4.** Cells per process = 4096²/16 = **1,048,576** [1]. 1D slabs (strips of 256 rows): an interior process exchanges two rows of 4096 = **8,192** halo cells per step [1.5]. 2D blocks (1024 × 1024): four edges of 1024 = **4,096** halo cells [1.5]. The 2D decomposition halves the communication volume (it scales with the perimeter, 4n/√p, rather than 2n) and the ratio of halo to interior is 0.39 percent; but it needs more messages (4 neighbours) so latency matters for small blocks [1].\n\n**B5.** Large blocking sends do not complete until the receiver posts a matching receive (no buffering); both ranks block in MPI_Send and neither reaches MPI_Recv: **deadlock** [2]. Fixes (any two): MPI_Sendrecv; non-blocking MPI_Isend/MPI_Irecv followed by MPI_Waitall; order the calls by rank (even ranks send first, odd ranks receive first); buffered send with sufficient buffer [3].\n\n**B6.** [2,3,1] ≤ [2,4,1] componentwise and not equal: the first **happened before** the second [2.5]. [3,1,0] and [2,2,0]: the first is larger in P1 and smaller in P2, so they are **concurrent** [2.5].\n\n**B7.** R + W = 8 > 7, so read quorums intersect write quorums: **yes**, reads see the latest acknowledged write [2]. Reads and writes each need 4 live replicas, so each tolerates up to **3 failures** [3].\n\n**B8.** k = (m/n) ln 2 = 16 × 0.693 = 11.09 → **11**; false-positive rate = (1 - e^(-11 × 500/8000))^11 = (1 - e^(-0.6875))^11 = (0.4972)^11 = **0.046 percent** (4.6×10⁻⁴) [3]. For m = 1,000, n = 100, k = 3: (1 - e^(-0.3))³ = (0.2592)³ = **1.74 percent** [2].\n\n### Section C\n\n**C1.**\n(a) Speedup = 200/t: p = 2: **1.92** (E 0.96, e 0.040); p = 4: **3.45** (E 0.86, e 0.053); p = 8: **5.56** (E 0.69, e 0.063) [5].\n(b) e rises slowly from 0.04 to 0.063, so there is a small inherent serial part plus overhead that grows with p (communication, synchronization, load imbalance, memory-bandwidth contention). Remedies: overlap communication with computation, reduce synchronization, improve load balance, increase work per process (weak scaling), and optimize memory access [3].\n(c) S(16) = 1/(e + (1 - e)/16) = 1/(0.063 + 0.0586) = **8.2** (efficiency 0.51) [2]. (If e keeps rising, the real value will be lower.)\n\n**C2.**\n(a) Total = 0 + 1 + ... + 199 = 19,900; ideal per thread 4,975. Static block (50 consecutive iterations each): loads **1,225, 3,725, 6,225, 8,725**; the time is set by the largest load 8,725: speedup 19,900/8,725 = **2.28** (imbalance 1.75). Cyclic: loads **4,900, 4,950, 5,000, 5,050**; time 5,050: speedup **3.94** (imbalance 1.02) [5].\n(b) The four counters occupy one 64-byte cache line (32 bytes), so every increment by one thread invalidates the line in the other cores' caches: **false sharing**, which can slow the loop by a factor of two or more (we measured 2.2×). Fixes: pad each counter to its own cache line; or keep a private local counter per thread and merge after the loop (reduction) [3].\n(c) `reduction(+:sum)` [2].\n\n**C3.**\n(a) Lamport: P1: a = 1, send m1 = 2, b = 3, receive m3 = max(3, 6) + 1 = 7. P2: receive m1 = 3, send m2 = 4. P3: c = 1, receive m2 = max(1, 4) + 1 = 5, send m3 = 6 [4].\n(b) Vector clocks [P1, P2, P3]: a [1,0,0]; send m1 [2,0,0]; P2 receive m1 [2,1,0]; send m2 [2,2,0]; c [0,0,1]; P3 receive m2 [2,2,2]; b [3,0,0]; send m3 [2,2,3]; P1 receive m3 [4,2,3] [4].\n(c) b = [3,0,0] and c = [0,0,1] are incomparable: **concurrent**. a = [1,0,0] ≤ [4,2,3]: **a happened before** the receive of m3 [2].\n\n**C4.**\n(a) P(at least 3 of 5 up) = Σ_{k=3}^{5} C(5,k) 0.9^k 0.1^(5-k) = 0.0729 + 0.32805 + 0.59049 = **0.99144** [2].\n(b) A majority is 3 of 5. Sorted indices 12, 12, 10, 9, 4: the third-highest is **10**, so entries up to index 10 are stored on at least three servers and can be committed (provided the entry at index 10 is from the leader's current term) [2].\n(c) Servers start as followers; if no heartbeat arrives before a randomized election timeout, a follower becomes a candidate, increments its term, votes for itself and requests votes; a server grants one vote per term and only to a candidate whose log is at least as up to date; a candidate with a **majority** becomes leader and sends heartbeats. A majority is needed because any two majorities intersect, so two leaders can never both be elected in one term and every committed entry survives into the next leader's log [3].\n(d) Ledger: **CP**: refuse or delay operations that cannot reach a quorum rather than risk inconsistent balances. Recommendation cache: **AP**: serve possibly stale data during a partition and reconcile later because staleness is harmless [3].\n\n**C5.**\n(a) Data = 10⁹ × 1 KB = 1 TB per copy; × 3 replicas = **3 TB**. At 70 percent of 1 TB per node, 3/0.7 = 4.29, so at least **5 nodes** [3].\n(b) Writes: 100,000 × 0.2 = 20,000/s, each going to 3 replicas = 60,000 node ops/s. Reads: 80,000/s, each querying 2 replicas = 160,000 node ops/s. Total **220,000 node operations/s**; across 6 nodes about **36,700 per node per second** (check that a node can sustain it; add nodes or caches if not) [3].\n(c) Consistent hashing places each node at many pseudo-random positions (virtual nodes, for example 128 per node = 768 positions for 6 nodes) on a hash ring; a key's replicas are the next 3 distinct nodes clockwise (the preference list). Virtual nodes smooth the load and spread a failed node's keys over many survivors. A new node takes over only about 1/N of the keys from its neighbours by streaming data; a failed node is detected by gossip/heartbeats and its replicas serve requests; **hinted handoff** stores writes for a temporarily down replica and delivers them later; **read repair** and anti-entropy (Merkle trees) fix divergence. With R = W = 2 and N = 3, R + W > N so reads see the latest write and the system tolerates one failed replica for both operations [4].\n","notes":"# Parallel and Distributed Computing: Complete Lecture Notes\n\nPrerequisites: computer architecture, operating systems, data structures and algorithms, computer networks and probability. Measurements marked \"measured\" were produced by compiling and running C programs (pthreads, OpenMP) under gcc on a 4-logical-core Linux machine (WSL); your times will differ, but the lessons will not. All other numbers (speedup laws, clocks, hashing, quorums, Bloom filters) were computed with short Python programs. MPI and CUDA code in these notes is shown for study and was not executed in the environment used to prepare them; no timing is claimed for it.\n\n## Chapter 1. Why Parallel and Distributed Computing\n\n### 1.1 The end of free speedups\nClock frequencies stopped rising around 2005 because of the power wall (dynamic power P = C V² f). Performance now comes from **parallelism**: multiple cores, SIMD vector units, GPUs with thousands of lanes, clusters and data centres. Programs must be written (or compiled) to expose parallel work.\n\n### 1.2 Parallel versus distributed\n- **Parallel computing**: several processing elements cooperate on one problem to finish sooner, usually with shared memory or a fast interconnect; the goal is **performance**.\n- **Distributed computing**: autonomous computers connected by a network cooperate and appear as one system; the goals are **scale, availability and geographic placement**. Failures, unreliable messages and the lack of a global clock are first-class problems.\n- **Concurrency** is the composition of independently executing tasks (it may happen on one core); **parallelism** is their simultaneous execution.\n\n### 1.3 Flynn's taxonomy and memory models\nSISD, SIMD (vector units, GPUs' warps), MISD (rare), MIMD (multicore, clusters). Memory: **shared memory** (threads see the same address space; need synchronization and cache coherence), **distributed memory** (each process owns its memory; communicate by messages), and hybrids (MPI between nodes, OpenMP or threads within a node, GPUs as accelerators). NUMA machines have different access times to local and remote memory.\n\n## Chapter 2. Performance Models\n\n### 2.1 Speedup and efficiency\nSpeedup S(p) = T(1)/T(p); efficiency E(p) = S(p)/p. Linear speedup (S = p) is the ideal; superlinear speedup can occur through cache effects.\n\n### 2.2 Amdahl's law\nIf a fraction f of the work can be parallelized and the rest is serial, S(p) = 1/((1 - f) + f/p), bounded by 1/(1 - f). Computed values for f = 0.5, 0.9, 0.95, 0.99:\n- f = 0.5: S(4) = 1.60, S(16) = 1.88, limit 2.\n- f = 0.9: S(4) = 3.08, S(16) = 6.40, S(64) = 8.77, limit 10.\n- f = 0.95: S(4) = 3.48, S(16) = 9.14, S(64) = 15.4, limit 20; efficiency at p = 16 is only 57 percent.\n- f = 0.99: S(16) = 13.9, S(64) = 39.3, S(1024) = 91.2, limit 100.\nTo reach S = 8 on 16 processors you need f = (1 - 1/8)/(1 - 1/16) = 0.933. Lesson: the serial fraction dominates; attack it first.\n\n### 2.3 Gustafson's law\nFor a problem that grows with the machine, scaled speedup is S = p - s(p - 1) where s is the serial fraction of the parallel run. With s = 0.05: 15.25 on 16 processors and 60.85 on 64. Strong scaling (fixed problem) follows Amdahl; **weak scaling** (problem size grows with p) follows Gustafson.\n\n### 2.4 Karp-Flatt metric\nFrom a measured speedup ψ on p processors the experimentally determined serial fraction is e = (1/ψ - 1/p)/(1 - 1/p). A measured speedup of 5.3 on 8 processors gives e = 0.073; 3.2 on 4 processors gives e = 0.083. If e stays constant as p grows, the limit is an inherent serial part; if e grows, parallel overhead (communication, load imbalance, contention) is the cause.\n\n**Measured example.** An OpenMP reduction over 4×10⁸ iterations of sin²(i) + cos²(i) took 39.08 s on 1 thread, 24.73 s on 2 (speedup 1.58, efficiency 0.79), 20.41 s on 3 (1.91, 0.64) and 19.87 s on 4 (1.97, 0.49). The Karp-Flatt value at p = 4 is (1/1.97 - 1/4)/(1 - 1/4) = **0.34**, far above what any serial code in the loop could explain: the four \"cores\" were hardware threads sharing execution units and the memory system (simultaneous multithreading), so they behave like about two physical cores. Always check what your processors really are.\n\n### 2.5 Work-span (DAG) model\nModel a computation as a DAG: **work** T₁ is the total number of operations, **span** T∞ the longest dependency chain. Parallelism = T₁/T∞. Brent's theorem: max(T₁/p, T∞) ≤ T_p ≤ T₁/p + T∞. Example: T₁ = 1000 and T∞ = 50 gives parallelism 20; on p = 4 the time is between 250 and 300; on p = 16 between 62.5 and 112.5; on p = 64 between 50 and 65.6: beyond about 20 processors extra hardware barely helps. Parallel reduction of n = 1024 elements has work 1023 and span log₂ 1024 = 10. Parallel merge sort of n = 2²⁰ elements has work ≈ n lg n = 2.1×10⁷ and span ≈ lg² n = 400 with a parallel merge, so parallelism is about 52,000 (plenty).\n\n### 2.6 Roofline model\nAttainable performance = min(peak FLOP/s, bandwidth × arithmetic intensity), with intensity in FLOP per byte. For a machine with 200 GFLOP/s peak and 50 GB/s memory bandwidth the ridge point is 4 FLOP/byte. DAXPY (y = a·x + y) does 2 FLOP per 24 bytes (0.083 FLOP/byte) and reaches only 4.2 GFLOP/s: **memory bound**. A blocked matrix multiply with 64×64 tiles has intensity 2·64³/(3·64²·8) = 5.3 FLOP/byte and can reach the 200 GFLOP/s peak: **compute bound**. Optimization goal: raise intensity (blocking, fusion, data reuse) before adding cores.\n\n### 2.7 Isoefficiency and scalability\nSumming n numbers on p processors with a tree reduction takes T_p ≈ n/p + 2 log₂ p (compute plus communication): for n = 2²⁰, p = 16 the speedup is 16.0 (efficiency ≈ 1); for p = 1024 it is 1004 (0.98); with n = 2²⁶ and p = 1024 it is 1023.7. A parallel algorithm is **scalable** if efficiency can be held constant by growing the problem with p (isoefficiency function).\n\n## Chapter 3. Shared-Memory Programming\n\n### 3.1 Threads and the race condition\nA **data race** occurs when two threads access the same location, at least one writes, and there is no synchronization. `counter++` is load, add, store, so concurrent increments can overwrite each other. **Measured:** four threads each incrementing a shared long one million times gave final values of 3,980,825, 3,980,110 and 3,985,421 in three runs instead of 4,000,000 (about 0.4 to 0.5 percent of updates lost, different every run), while the same loop protected by a mutex or implemented with an atomic fetch-and-add gave exactly 4,000,000 every time.\n\n```\n#include <pthread.h>\n#include <stdatomic.h>\nlong counter = 0;                 /* racy */\natomic_long acounter = 0;         /* atomic */\npthread_mutex_t m = PTHREAD_MUTEX_INITIALIZER;\nvoid *work(void *a) {\n  for (int i = 0; i < 1000000; i++) {\n    counter++;                                   /* data race */\n    pthread_mutex_lock(&m); mcounter++; pthread_mutex_unlock(&m);\n    atomic_fetch_add(&acounter, 1);              /* lock-free */\n  }\n  return 0;\n}\n```\n\n### 3.2 Synchronization primitives\n- **Mutex / lock**: mutual exclusion around a critical section. Keep critical sections short.\n- **Condition variable**: wait for a predicate (always in a `while` loop; use with a mutex).\n- **Semaphore**: counting permits (bounded buffers).\n- **Barrier**: all threads wait until everyone arrives. **Reader-writer lock**, **spinlock**, **atomic operations** (compare-and-swap, fetch-and-add), **memory fences**.\n- **Producer-consumer** with a bounded buffer: two semaphores (empty slots, full slots) and a mutex.\n\n### 3.3 Deadlock, livelock and starvation\nDeadlock needs the four Coffman conditions: mutual exclusion, hold-and-wait, no preemption and circular wait. Thread 1 locks A then B while thread 2 locks B then A: a cycle. Prevention: a **global lock ordering** (always lock lower-numbered first), try-lock with back-off, acquiring all locks at once, or avoiding locks (lock-free structures, message passing). **Livelock**: threads keep reacting to each other without progress. **Starvation**: a thread never gets the resource (use fair locks).\n\n### 3.4 Memory consistency and cache coherence\nHardware caches keep copies; the **coherence protocol** (MESI) invalidates copies on writes. The **memory model** defines the order in which writes become visible; sequential consistency is the intuitive model, but real CPUs and compilers reorder operations, so correct programs use atomics with acquire/release semantics. A program free of data races behaves as if sequentially consistent (in C11/C++11/Java).\n\n**False sharing.** Threads writing different variables that share one 64-byte cache line make the line bounce between cores. **Measured:** four threads each incrementing their own counter 2×10⁸ times took 2.02 s when the counters were adjacent in an array and 0.91 s when padded to separate cache lines: **2.2× slower purely from false sharing**. Fix by padding or by accumulating in a local variable and writing once.\n\n### 3.5 OpenMP\nCompiler directives for loops and tasks:\n```\ndouble s = 0;\n#pragma omp parallel for reduction(+:s)\nfor (long i = 0; i < N; i++) s += f(i);\n```\nKey ideas: `parallel`, `for`, `reduction`, `private`/`shared`, `schedule(static|dynamic|guided)`, `critical`, `atomic`, `barrier`, `task`. Static scheduling suits uniform iterations; dynamic suits irregular ones. The reduction clause gives each thread a private partial sum and combines at the end, avoiding the race.\n\n### 3.6 Thread pools, futures and task parallelism\nCreating a thread per task is expensive; use a **thread pool** with a work queue, **work stealing** (Cilk, Java ForkJoin, Go's scheduler, Rust's rayon) to balance irregular tasks, and **futures/promises** for results. Prefer high-level patterns: map, reduce, pipeline, fork-join, divide and conquer.\n\n### 3.7 Lock-free ideas\nCompare-and-swap (CAS) loops build lock-free stacks and queues; the **ABA problem** (a value changes A→B→A so CAS wrongly succeeds) is handled with version tags or hazard pointers. Lock-free does not mean faster; it avoids blocking and priority inversion.\n\n## Chapter 4. Parallel Algorithms and Patterns\n\n- **Reduction**: tree of depth log₂ n; floating-point sums are not associative, so results can differ between runs and thread counts.\n- **Prefix sum (scan)**: the inclusive scan of 3, 1, 7, 0, 4, 1, 6, 3 is 3, 4, 11, 11, 15, 16, 22, 25. For n = 16 the Hillis-Steele scan takes log₂ n = 4 steps but does 49 additions; the work-efficient Blelloch scan does 2(n - 1) = 30 additions in 2 log₂ n = 8 steps. Scan underlies stream compaction, radix sort, allocation and many GPU algorithms.\n- **Matrix multiplication**: 2n³ FLOP; for n = 4096 that is 1.37×10¹¹ FLOP (1.37 s at 100 GFLOP/s). Block (tile) it for cache; distribute by 2D blocks (Cannon, SUMMA) to reduce communication.\n- **Stencil / grid codes**: partition the grid, exchange **halo (ghost) cells** with neighbours each step; communication scales with the boundary (surface), computation with the volume, so larger subdomains are more efficient.\n- **Sorting**: parallel merge sort, sample sort (choose splitters, bucket, local sort), bitonic sort on GPUs.\n- **Graph algorithms**: breadth-first search level by level; irregular, load-imbalanced and bandwidth-bound.\n- **Load balancing**: static partitioning for regular work; dynamic scheduling or work stealing for irregular work. Imbalance gives idle processors at barriers.\n\n## Chapter 5. Message Passing and Distributed-Memory Programming\n\n### 5.1 Communication cost model\nSending m bytes costs T = α + β m (latency plus bandwidth term). With α = 1 µs and β = 1 ns/byte: a 1 KB message takes 2 µs (latency-dominated) and a 1 MB message 1.001 ms (bandwidth-dominated). Therefore aggregate small messages. A linear broadcast of 1 KB to 64 processes costs 63(α + βm) = 126 µs; a binomial-tree broadcast needs only log₂ 64 = 6 steps = 12 µs. A ring all-reduce of 100 MB over 8 processes costs 2(p - 1)α + 2(p - 1)/p · mβ = 0.175 s and is nearly independent of p for large messages (bandwidth-optimal).\n\n### 5.2 MPI\nProcesses with ranks in a communicator; point-to-point `MPI_Send`/`MPI_Recv` (blocking), `MPI_Isend`/`MPI_Irecv` (non-blocking, to overlap communication with computation), and collectives `MPI_Bcast`, `MPI_Scatter`, `MPI_Gather`, `MPI_Reduce`, `MPI_Allreduce`, `MPI_Alltoall`, `MPI_Barrier`.\n```\n/* study example, not executed in the preparation environment */\nMPI_Init(&argc, &argv);\nMPI_Comm_rank(MPI_COMM_WORLD, &rank);\ndouble local = partial_sum(rank, size), total;\nMPI_Reduce(&local, &total, 1, MPI_DOUBLE, MPI_SUM, 0, MPI_COMM_WORLD);\nif (rank == 0) printf(\"%f\\n\", total);\nMPI_Finalize();\n```\nDeadlock risk: two ranks both calling blocking `MPI_Send` to each other before receiving can deadlock when messages exceed the buffering; use `MPI_Sendrecv`, non-blocking calls or an ordering convention.\n\n### 5.3 Hybrid and data-parallel training\nTypical hybrid: MPI between nodes, OpenMP within. In **data-parallel deep learning** every worker computes gradients on its mini-batch and an all-reduce averages them. If compute takes 100 ms and a non-overlapped all-reduce 20 ms, efficiency is 100/120 = 0.83; overlapping communication with the backward pass leaves 5 ms exposed and efficiency 0.95.\n\n## Chapter 6. GPUs and Accelerators\n\n### 6.1 Execution model\nA GPU has many streaming multiprocessors (SMs); a kernel launches a **grid** of **blocks** of **threads**; threads run in **warps** of 32 in lock-step (SIMT). Divergent branches inside a warp execute both paths serially. Memory hierarchy: registers, shared memory (per block, fast), L2, global memory (high latency and bandwidth). **Coalesced** accesses (consecutive threads reading consecutive addresses) combine into few transactions; strided accesses waste bandwidth.\n```\n/* study example, not executed in the preparation environment */\n__global__ void saxpy(int n, float a, const float *x, float *y) {\n  int i = blockIdx.x * blockDim.x + threadIdx.x;\n  if (i < n) y[i] = a * x[i] + y[i];\n}\n/* launch: saxpy<<<(n + 255) / 256, 256>>>(n, a, x, y); */\n```\nFor n = 10⁶ and 256 threads per block you need ⌈10⁶/256⌉ = **3,907 blocks**, each of 8 warps.\n\n### 6.2 Occupancy\nResident threads per SM are limited by threads (2,048), registers (65,536) and shared memory. With 256-thread blocks and 40 registers per thread, an SM holds min(2048/256, 65536/(40·256)) = 6 blocks: occupancy 6·256/2048 = **75 percent**; at 64 registers per thread only 4 blocks fit: occupancy **50 percent**. High occupancy helps hide memory latency, but is not the only goal.\n\n### 6.3 When GPUs win\nLarge, data-parallel workloads with high arithmetic intensity and regular access. Costs: transferring data over PCIe (about 16 to 32 GB/s) can dominate small problems; use pinned memory, streams and overlap. Programming models: CUDA, HIP, OpenCL, SYCL, OpenACC; libraries (cuBLAS, cuDNN, Thrust) are usually better than hand-written kernels.\n\n## Chapter 7. Distributed Systems Fundamentals\n\n### 7.1 Models and challenges\nProcesses communicate by messages that can be delayed, lost, duplicated or reordered; processes can crash (fail-stop) or behave arbitrarily (Byzantine). There is no shared clock and no global state. Timing models: synchronous (bounded delays), asynchronous (unbounded), partially synchronous (eventually bounded, the realistic assumption). Failure detectors are therefore unreliable (a slow node looks dead).\n\n### 7.2 Time and ordering\n**Lamport clocks**: each process increments its counter on every event; on receiving a message sets clock = max(local, message) + 1. If a happened-before b then L(a) < L(b), but not conversely. Example with processes P1, P2, P3: P1: a = 1, send m1 = 2; P2: recv m1 = 3, b = 4, send m2 = 5; P3: c = 1, recv m2 = 6; P1: d = 3. Events a (stamp 1) and c (stamp 1) carry equal stamps, and d (3) at P1 has the same stamp as the receive of m1 at P2 (3), although these pairs are concurrent: Lamport stamps order events consistently with causality but cannot reveal concurrency.\n**Vector clocks** keep one counter per process and detect concurrency: the same run gives P1: a [1,0,0], send [2,0,0]; P2: recv [2,1,0], b [2,2,0], send [2,3,0]; P3: c [0,0,1], recv [2,3,2]; P1: d [3,0,0]. Event c [0,0,1] and a [1,0,0] are incomparable, hence **concurrent**; a [1,0,0] < recv m1 [2,1,0], hence a happened before it. Physical clocks (NTP, PTP, GPS) drift; Google Spanner's TrueTime exposes clock uncertainty and waits it out.\n\n### 7.3 Consistency models and CAP\nStrong (linearizable) consistency behaves as if there is one copy; sequential, causal and **eventual consistency** are weaker and cheaper. The **CAP theorem**: during a network **partition** a system must choose between **consistency** and **availability**; without partitions it can have both, but there is still a latency versus consistency trade-off (PACELC). CP systems (ZooKeeper, etcd, Spanner) refuse some requests during a partition; AP systems (Dynamo, Cassandra, Riak) stay available and reconcile later (version vectors, last-write-wins, CRDTs).\n\n### 7.4 Replication and quorums\nWith N replicas, a write waits for W acknowledgements and a read queries R replicas. If **R + W > N** every read quorum intersects every write quorum, so reads see the latest write. Examples: (N, R, W) = (3, 2, 2) tolerates one failed replica for both reads and writes; (3, 1, 3) gives fast reads but no write tolerance; (5, 3, 3) tolerates two failures; (5, 2, 2) violates R + W > N and can return stale data. Availability: with each node up 99 percent of the time, a majority of 3 is available with probability 0.99970 and a majority of 5 with 0.99999.\n\n### 7.5 Consensus\nConsensus (agree on one value despite crashes) is impossible in a purely asynchronous system with even one crash (**FLP result**), but practical protocols work under partial synchrony.\n- **Paxos**: proposers, acceptors, learners; phase 1 (prepare/promise) and phase 2 (accept/accepted); safe with any minority of failures. Multi-Paxos elects a stable leader.\n- **Raft**: understandable consensus with leader election, log replication and safety. Servers are followers, candidates or leaders. A follower becoming a candidate after a randomized election timeout (for example 150 to 300 ms) requests votes; a majority wins. Randomization avoids split votes: with 4 followers drawing timeouts uniformly from 150 to 300 ms, the chance that the two earliest timeouts are within 10 ms of each other (a risk of a split vote) was about **24 percent** in a simulation, so retries are normal and a longer range lowers it. A leader appends entries and commits once a majority stores them; N = 5 tolerates 2 failures.\n- **Byzantine fault tolerance** needs n ≥ 3f + 1 replicas to tolerate f arbitrary faults: 4 for f = 1, 7 for f = 2 (PBFT, blockchains).\n\n### 7.6 Atomic commit\n**Two-phase commit (2PC)**: the coordinator sends prepare; participants vote yes/no; if all yes, the coordinator sends commit, otherwise abort. With 4 participants a successful 2PC exchanges 4 prepares, 4 votes, 4 commits and 4 acks = 16 messages. It **blocks** if the coordinator fails after participants voted yes; three-phase commit and consensus-based commit (Paxos Commit) reduce blocking. Alternatives: **sagas** (a sequence of local transactions with compensating actions) for microservices.\n\n### 7.7 Partitioning and consistent hashing\nHash partitioning with `hash(key) mod N` is simple, but changing N from 4 to 5 remaps about 80 percent of keys (measured on 20,000 keys: 80.3 percent). **Consistent hashing** places nodes and keys on a ring; a key belongs to the next node clockwise, so adding a node moves only about 1/N of the keys: measured 16.9 percent of keys moved with one point per node and 14.5 percent with 100 **virtual nodes** per node, close to the ideal 20 percent (the new fifth node should take one fifth of the keys). Virtual nodes also even out the load: the ratio of most to least loaded node fell from 2.86 to 1.64 in the same experiment.\n\n### 7.8 Gossip, membership and probabilistic structures\n**Gossip** spreads information epidemically: each round each node tells a few random peers; full dissemination takes O(log N) rounds (about log₂ N + ln N ≈ 17 rounds for N = 1000). A **Bloom filter** answers \"possibly in set / definitely not\". With m = 10,000 bits, n = 1,000 items and the optimal k = (m/n) ln 2 ≈ 7 hash functions, the false-positive rate is (1 - e^(-kn/m))^k = **0.82 percent**; measured on 20,000 absent items: 0.86 percent. No false negatives; no deletions in the basic form. Others: HyperLogLog (cardinality), count-min sketch (frequencies).\n\n## Chapter 8. Distributed Data Processing\n\n### 8.1 MapReduce\nSplit the input, **map** each record to key-value pairs, **shuffle** by key, **reduce** each group. Word count of \"the quick brown fox / the lazy dog / the fox\" gives the=3, fox=2, quick=1, brown=1, lazy=1, dog=1. Keys are partitioned by hash (with three reducers: the, brown, lazy → 0; quick → 1; fox, dog → 2 in our run). Combiners pre-aggregate on the map side to cut shuffle traffic. Fault tolerance: re-execute failed tasks; stragglers are handled by speculative execution. Spark keeps data in memory as RDDs/DataFrames with lineage for recovery, making iterative algorithms far faster.\n\n### 8.2 Distributed storage\nDistributed file systems (HDFS: large blocks, 3 replicas, NameNode metadata), object stores (S3), key-value stores (Dynamo-style with consistent hashing and quorums), wide-column and document stores, distributed SQL (Spanner, CockroachDB). Use **log-structured** storage (LSM trees) for write-heavy loads, and replicate across failure domains (rack, zone, region).\n\n### 8.3 Stream processing and messaging\nMessage logs (Kafka) provide ordered, replayable partitions; consumers track offsets. Delivery semantics: at-most-once, at-least-once (retries; make handlers **idempotent**), exactly-once (idempotence + transactions). Stream engines (Flink, Spark Structured Streaming) handle event time, windows and watermarks.\n\n## Chapter 9. Performance, Reliability and Operations\n\n### 9.1 Queueing and tail latency\n**Little's law**: L = λW; 200 requests/s with 50 ms latency means 10 requests in the system. For an M/M/1 queue with service rate μ = 100/s, the mean response time 1/(μ - λ) is 33 ms at 70 percent utilization, 50 ms at 80 percent and **100 ms at 90 percent**: latency explodes near saturation, so keep utilization below about 70 percent. **Tail at scale:** if a request fans out to 100 servers and each is slow 1 percent of the time, the chance that at least one is slow is 1 - 0.99¹⁰⁰ = **63 percent** (9.6 percent for a fan-out of 10). Mitigate with hedged requests, timeouts and replicas.\n\n### 9.2 Fault tolerance techniques\nReplication, checkpointing and restart (HPC), retries with exponential backoff and jitter, circuit breakers, idempotent operations, timeouts everywhere, load shedding and graceful degradation, chaos engineering. In HPC, the checkpoint interval balancing overhead and lost work is about √(2 · checkpoint cost · MTBF) (Young's formula).\n\n### 9.3 Observability and testing\nMetrics, logs and traces (distributed tracing with correlation IDs); test with fault injection, network partitions and clock skew (Jepsen-style); use deterministic simulation where possible. Data races: ThreadSanitizer (`gcc -fsanitize=thread`), Helgrind; deadlocks: lock-order analysis.\n\n## Chapter 10. Design Method and Mistakes Checklist\n1. Measure the serial program first and profile; find the hot spots and the memory-versus-compute bound (roofline).\n2. Estimate the achievable speedup with Amdahl/Gustafson and the work-span parallelism before coding.\n3. Choose the model: shared memory (OpenMP/threads) for one node, MPI across nodes, GPU for data-parallel kernels, a framework (Spark) for data processing, consensus-based systems for coordination.\n4. Minimize sharing and communication; make data local; prefer immutable data and message passing.\n5. Verify with race detectors and compare against the serial result (allowing for floating-point reassociation).\n6. Measure speedup and efficiency, use Karp-Flatt to diagnose.\n\n**Common mistakes.** Forgetting that unsynchronized shared counters lose updates (measured 0.4 to 0.5 percent here); locking too coarsely or in inconsistent orders; false sharing; assuming hardware threads are full cores (our 4-thread speedup was only 1.97); parallelizing a loop whose iterations depend on each other; ignoring load imbalance and communication latency; using `mod N` hashing for resizable clusters; confusing Lamport timestamps with a total causal order; assuming a timeout means a node is dead; retrying non-idempotent operations; and reporting speedup against a slow baseline.\n","plan":"# Parallel and Distributed Computing: 14-Week Study Plan and Resources\n\nTime budget: 9 to 12 hours per week (3 reading or lectures, 6 to 8 programming and measuring, 1 review with the app's quizzes). Every concept in this course can be tested on your own machine: write the serial code, measure it, parallelize it, then measure again and explain the gap with Amdahl's law and the Karp-Flatt metric.\n\n## Textbooks\n- Peter Pacheco and Matthew Malensky, *An Introduction to Parallel Programming* (MPI, Pthreads, OpenMP, CUDA).\n- Michael McCool, Arch Robison and James Reinders, *Structured Parallel Programming* (patterns); Maurice Herlihy and Nir Shavit, *The Art of Multiprocessor Programming* (locks, lock-free structures, memory models).\n- Ananth Grama, Anshul Gupta, George Karypis and Vipin Kumar, *Introduction to Parallel Computing* (analysis, isoefficiency).\n- Martin Kleppmann, *Designing Data-Intensive Applications* (replication, partitioning, consensus, streams): the best practical book for the distributed half.\n- Andrew Tanenbaum and Maarten van Steen, *Distributed Systems*; George Coulouris et al., *Distributed Systems: Concepts and Design*; Leslie Lamport's papers on time, clocks and Paxos.\n- David Kirk and Wen-mei Hwu, *Programming Massively Parallel Processors* (GPUs).\n\n## Free courses and resources\n- MIT 6.824 Distributed Systems (lectures, labs on Raft and key-value stores); MIT 6.172 Performance Engineering; Stanford CS149 Parallel Computing.\n- Lawrence Livermore National Laboratory tutorials on OpenMP, MPI and POSIX threads (free, concise).\n- The Raft visualization and paper (\"In Search of an Understandable Consensus Algorithm\"); the Dynamo, Bigtable, Spanner, MapReduce and Chubby papers.\n- NVIDIA CUDA programming guide and \"An Even Easier Introduction to CUDA\"; Kaggle or Colab free GPUs.\n- Jepsen.io analyses of real databases; the \"Fallacies of distributed computing\" essay.\n\n## Tools\ngcc/clang with `-fopenmp` and `-pthread`; ThreadSanitizer (`-fsanitize=thread`); `perf`, Valgrind/Helgrind; OpenMPI or MPICH (`mpicc`, `mpirun`); CUDA toolkit or a Colab notebook; Python (`multiprocessing`, `concurrent.futures`, Dask); Docker for multi-node simulations; Go or Rust for concurrency; `tc netem` to add latency and loss to a test network.\n\n## Week by week\n\n**Week 1: Motivation and architectures.** Power wall, multicore, Flynn's taxonomy, shared versus distributed memory, concurrency versus parallelism. Lab: query your CPU (`lscpu`), find physical cores versus hardware threads, and time a serial benchmark.\n\n**Week 2: Performance models.** Speedup, efficiency, Amdahl, Gustafson, Karp-Flatt, work-span, roofline, isoefficiency. Lab: write a table of measured speedups and compute Karp-Flatt for each p.\n\n**Week 3: Threads and synchronization.** pthreads, mutexes, condition variables, semaphores, barriers, atomics. Lab: reproduce the racy counter (watch the final value vary) and fix it three ways.\n\n**Week 4: Correctness hazards.** Deadlock, livelock, starvation, lock ordering, memory models and ordering, false sharing, the ABA problem. Lab: create and fix a deadlock; measure false sharing with padded and unpadded counters.\n\n**Week 5: OpenMP.** Parallel loops, reductions, schedules, tasks, data-sharing clauses, NUMA effects. Lab: parallelize a numerical integration and a stencil; compare static, dynamic and guided schedules on an irregular loop.\n\n**Week 6: Parallel algorithms.** Reduction, scan, sorting, matrix multiplication, stencils, graph traversal, load balancing. Lab: implement prefix scan and parallel merge sort; measure work and span.\n\n**Week 7: Midterm and message passing.** Review. Communication cost model, MPI basics, point-to-point and collectives. Lab: ping-pong benchmark to estimate α and β; compare broadcast algorithms.\n\n**Week 8: MPI applications.** Domain decomposition, halo exchange, non-blocking communication, deadlock avoidance, hybrid MPI + OpenMP. Lab: a 2D heat equation solver with halo exchange; measure strong and weak scaling.\n\n**Week 9: GPUs.** CUDA model (grid, block, warp), memory hierarchy, coalescing, occupancy, divergence, streams. Lab: SAXPY, reduction and tiled matrix multiplication; compare with a CPU baseline honestly (include transfer time).\n\n**Week 10: Distributed systems fundamentals.** Failure and timing models, logical and vector clocks, consistency models, CAP/PACELC. Lab: implement Lamport and vector clocks over a toy message simulator.\n\n**Week 11: Replication and consensus.** Quorums, leader election, Paxos and Raft, Byzantine faults, two-phase commit, sagas. Lab: run a Raft implementation (etcd or MIT 6.824 lab 2) and kill the leader; observe elections.\n\n**Week 12: Partitioning and storage.** Consistent hashing, virtual nodes, Dynamo-style stores, LSM trees, gossip, Bloom filters. Lab: build a consistent-hash ring and measure the fraction of keys that move when a node joins.\n\n**Week 13: Data processing and operations.** MapReduce, Spark, Kafka-style logs, delivery semantics, idempotence, tail latency, queueing, chaos testing. Lab: a word-count job in Spark or Dask; inject faults and observe recovery.\n\n**Week 14: Integration project and review.** Final project: a parallel or distributed application with a performance study (speedup, efficiency, Karp-Flatt, roofline or queueing analysis) and a fault test. Past-paper revision.\n\n## Assessment pattern used in this course\nThree assignments (performance models and scalability; shared-memory programming and GPUs; distributed systems), a class test, and a three-hour final. Practise by reproducing each worked number in the notes in code and then changing the parameters.\n\n## Study tips\n- Profile before parallelizing and measure after: never claim speedup without a serial baseline and the machine's real core count.\n- Reduce sharing: private data, message passing and immutable structures remove most concurrency bugs.\n- Use race detectors (ThreadSanitizer) on every shared-memory program; run tests many times and with varied thread counts.\n- In distributed systems assume messages are delayed, lost and duplicated and that clocks disagree; design operations to be idempotent and retries to be safe.\n- Draw message diagrams for every protocol you study (2PC, Raft, Paxos) and replay failure cases by hand.\n","test":"# Parallel and Distributed Computing: Class Test\n\nDuration: 60 minutes. Calculator allowed, no notes. Total: 60 marks. Section A: 20 multiple-choice questions (1 mark each). Section B: 4 problems (10 marks each). Speedup S = T(1)/T(p); Amdahl S = 1/((1 - f) + f/p); Karp-Flatt e = (1/S - 1/p)/(1 - 1/p); Brent: max(T₁/p, T∞) ≤ T_p ≤ T₁/p + T∞.\n\n## Section A: Multiple choice (20 marks)\n\n**1.** Concurrency differs from parallelism in that concurrency\nA) is the composition of independently executing tasks, which may interleave on one core  B) is always faster  C) requires several processors  D) needs a GPU\n\n**2.** With parallel fraction f, Amdahl's law bounds the speedup by\nA) 1/(1 - f)  B) 1 + f  C) f  D) p\n\n**3.** Gustafson's law describes\nA) cache misses  B) scaled speedup when the problem grows with the machine  C) communication cost  D) the maximum speedup of a fixed problem\n\n**4.** A data race occurs when\nA) two threads access the same location, at least one writes, with no synchronization  B) a thread sleeps  C) two threads run on different cores  D) a lock is held too long\n\n**5.** A mutex provides\nA) a barrier  B) mutual exclusion for a critical section  C) faster memory  D) a bigger cache\n\n**6.** Which is NOT one of the Coffman conditions for deadlock?\nA) circular wait  B) mutual exclusion  C) hold and wait  D) unlimited memory\n\n**7.** False sharing happens when\nA) threads write different variables that lie on the same cache line  B) a GPU shares memory  C) two processes share a file  D) a lock is shared\n\n**8.** In OpenMP the clause reduction(+:sum) is used to\nA) give each thread a private partial sum that is combined at the end  B) shorten the loop  C) reduce the number of threads  D) remove the barrier\n\n**9.** Static block scheduling performs badly when\nA) there is one thread  B) the loop is empty  C) iteration costs vary strongly (for example cost proportional to i)  D) all iterations cost the same\n\n**10.** In the cost model T = α + βm, α represents\nA) bandwidth  B) the number of hops  C) message size  D) the fixed startup latency\n\n**11.** MPI_Allreduce\nA) sends a result only to rank 0  B) is a point-to-point send  C) leaves every process with the combined result  D) kills the job\n\n**12.** A GPU warp is\nA) a block of 1,024 threads  B) a group of 32 threads executing in lock-step  C) a memory type  D) a kernel\n\n**13.** Memory accesses are coalesced when\nA) threads access random addresses  B) consecutive threads access consecutive addresses  C) every thread reads the same address  D) accesses are strided by 32\n\n**14.** A property of Lamport clocks is\nA) they need a central server  B) if a happened before b then L(a) < L(b)  C) they detect concurrency  D) they equal wall-clock time\n\n**15.** Vector clocks, unlike Lamport clocks, can\nA) avoid messages  B) run faster  C) detect that two events are concurrent  D) replace NTP\n\n**16.** The CAP theorem says that during a network partition a system must choose between\nA) CPU and GPU  B) speed and size  C) cost and performance  D) consistency and availability\n\n**17.** With N replicas, read quorum R and write quorum W, reads see the latest write if\nA) R = 1 always  B) W = 0  C) R + W < N  D) R + W > N\n\n**18.** Raft uses randomized election timeouts to\nA) encrypt messages  B) avoid repeated split votes  C) compress logs  D) speed up the network\n\n**19.** The main benefit of consistent hashing is that when a node is added\nA) no hashing is needed  B) data are deleted  C) only a small fraction of keys move  D) all keys move\n\n**20.** An idempotent operation is one that\nA) has the same effect when repeated as when executed once  B) is always fast  C) must run exactly once  D) can never fail\n\n## Section B: Problems (40 marks)\n\n**B1. Speedup (10 marks)** (a) A program is 90 percent parallelizable. Compute the speedup on 10 and on 100 processors and the limit [4]. (b) A measured speedup is 4 on 6 processors; compute the Karp-Flatt serial fraction and interpret [3]. (c) A DAG has work 1,000 and span 40; give its parallelism and the Brent bounds for p = 10 [3].\n\n**B2. Shared memory (10 marks)** (a) Two threads each run `count++` 1,000 times on a shared counter without synchronization; explain why the result may be below 2,000 and give two fixes [4]. (b) Explain how locking A then B in one thread and B then A in another can deadlock and how to prevent it [3]. (c) Why does padding per-thread counters to 64 bytes speed up a program whose threads update adjacent counters? [3]\n\n**B3. Logical clocks (10 marks)** Events: P1: a; P1: send m (to P2); P2: receive m; P2: b; P3: c. (a) Give Lamport timestamps [3]. (b) Give vector clocks [3]. (c) Is c concurrent with a? Is a before b? Justify using vector clocks [4].\n\n**B4. Distributed data (10 marks)** (a) N = 3 replicas with R = 2, W = 2: do reads see the latest write? How many failures can writes tolerate? [3] (b) A ring (positions 0 to 99) has nodes X at 20 and Y at 60. Keys with hashes 10, 30, 70 are assigned to the first node at or above the hash going clockwise, wrapping around. Assign them; then add node Z at 80 and say which keys move [4] (c) An M/M/1 server has μ = 80 requests/s and λ = 60 requests/s: compute utilization, mean response time and mean number in the system [3].\n\n## Answer key and solutions\n\n### Section A\n1. A 2. A 3. B 4. A 5. B 6. D 7. A 8. A 9. C 10. D 11. C 12. B 13. B 14. B 15. C 16. D 17. D 18. B 19. C 20. A\n\n### Section B\n\n**B1.**\n(a) S(10) = 1/(0.1 + 0.09) = **5.26**; S(100) = 1/(0.1 + 0.009) = **9.17**; limit = 1/0.1 = **10** [4].\n(b) e = (1/4 - 1/6)/(1 - 1/6) = (0.25 - 0.1667)/0.8333 = **0.10**: about 10 percent of the work behaves as serial (inherent serial code or overhead); compare e at other values of p to see whether it grows (overhead) or stays constant (inherent serial fraction) [3].\n(c) Parallelism = 1000/40 = **25**. For p = 10: lower bound max(100, 40) = **100**; upper bound 100 + 40 = **140** [3].\n\n**B2.**\n(a) `count++` is load, add, store; if both threads load the same value before either stores, one increment is lost, so the final value can be less than 2,000 and varies from run to run [2]. Fixes (any two): a mutex around the increment, an atomic fetch-and-add, or per-thread counters summed after join (reduction) [2].\n(b) Thread 1 holds A and waits for B, thread 2 holds B and waits for A: circular wait, so both block forever [2]. Prevent by always acquiring locks in the same global order (A before B), or by try-lock with back-off [1].\n(c) Adjacent counters share a 64-byte cache line; every write by one core invalidates the line in the other cores' caches, so the line bounces between them (false sharing). Padding puts each counter on its own line, so cores update their lines without interference [3].\n\n**B3.**\n(a) Lamport: a = 1; send m = 2; receive m = max(0, 2) + 1 = 3; b = 4; c = 1 [3].\n(b) Vector clocks [P1, P2, P3]: a [1,0,0]; send m [2,0,0]; receive m [2,1,0]; b [2,2,0]; c [0,0,1] [3].\n(c) c = [0,0,1] and a = [1,0,0] are incomparable, so **c is concurrent with a** (their Lamport stamps are both 1, which alone could not show concurrency). a = [1,0,0] ≤ b = [2,2,0], so **a happened before b** [4].\n\n**B4.**\n(a) R + W = 4 > 3, so every read quorum overlaps every write quorum: reads see the latest write [2]. Writes need 2 of 3 replicas, so they tolerate **1** failure [1].\n(b) Key 10 → X (20); key 30 → Y (60); key 70 → no node at or above 70, wrap around to X (20). After adding Z at 80: key 70 → Z; keys 10 and 30 unchanged. Only **key 70 moves, from X to Z** [4].\n(c) ρ = 60/80 = **0.75**; W = 1/(80 - 60) = **50 ms**; L = λ/(μ - λ) = 60/20 = **3** [3].\n"},mcq:[{"q":"Concurrency differs from parallelism in that concurrency","options":["is the composition of independently executing tasks, which may interleave on one core","is always faster","requires several processors","needs a GPU"],"answer":0,"explain":"The correct answer is A.","f":0.025,"src":"Class test"},{"q":"With parallel fraction f, Amdahl's law bounds the speedup by","options":["1/(1 - f)","1 + f","f","p"],"answer":0,"explain":"The correct answer is A.","f":0.075,"src":"Class test"},{"q":"Gustafson's law describes","options":["cache misses","scaled speedup when the problem grows with the machine","communication cost","the maximum speedup of a fixed problem"],"answer":1,"explain":"The correct answer is B.","f":0.125,"src":"Class test"},{"q":"A data race occurs when","options":["two threads access the same location, at least one writes, with no synchronization","a thread sleeps","two threads run on different cores","a lock is held too long"],"answer":0,"explain":"The correct answer is A.","f":0.175,"src":"Class test"},{"q":"A mutex provides","options":["a barrier","mutual exclusion for a critical section","faster memory","a bigger cache"],"answer":1,"explain":"The correct answer is B.","f":0.225,"src":"Class test"},{"q":"Which is NOT one of the Coffman conditions for deadlock?","options":["circular wait","mutual exclusion","hold and wait","unlimited memory"],"answer":3,"explain":"The correct answer is D.","f":0.275,"src":"Class test"},{"q":"False sharing happens when","options":["threads write different variables that lie on the same cache line","a GPU shares memory","two processes share a file","a lock is shared"],"answer":0,"explain":"The correct answer is A.","f":0.325,"src":"Class test"},{"q":"In OpenMP the clause reduction(+:sum) is used to","options":["give each thread a private partial sum that is combined at the end","shorten the loop","reduce the number of threads","remove the barrier"],"answer":0,"explain":"The correct answer is A.","f":0.375,"src":"Class test"},{"q":"Static block scheduling performs badly when","options":["there is one thread","the loop is empty","iteration costs vary strongly (for example cost proportional to i)","all iterations cost the same"],"answer":2,"explain":"The correct answer is C.","f":0.425,"src":"Class test"},{"q":"In the cost model T = α + βm, α represents","options":["bandwidth","the number of hops","message size","the fixed startup latency"],"answer":3,"explain":"The correct answer is D.","f":0.475,"src":"Class test"},{"q":"MPI_Allreduce","options":["sends a result only to rank 0","is a point-to-point send","leaves every process with the combined result","kills the job"],"answer":2,"explain":"The correct answer is C.","f":0.525,"src":"Class test"},{"q":"A GPU warp is","options":["a block of 1,024 threads","a group of 32 threads executing in lock-step","a memory type","a kernel"],"answer":1,"explain":"The correct answer is B.","f":0.575,"src":"Class test"},{"q":"Memory accesses are coalesced when","options":["threads access random addresses","consecutive threads access consecutive addresses","every thread reads the same address","accesses are strided by 32"],"answer":1,"explain":"The correct answer is B.","f":0.625,"src":"Class test"},{"q":"A property of Lamport clocks is","options":["they need a central server","if a happened before b then L(a) < L(b)","they detect concurrency","they equal wall-clock time"],"answer":1,"explain":"The correct answer is B.","f":0.675,"src":"Class test"},{"q":"Vector clocks, unlike Lamport clocks, can","options":["avoid messages","run faster","detect that two events are concurrent","replace NTP"],"answer":2,"explain":"The correct answer is C.","f":0.725,"src":"Class test"},{"q":"The CAP theorem says that during a network partition a system must choose between","options":["CPU and GPU","speed and size","cost and performance","consistency and availability"],"answer":3,"explain":"The correct answer is D.","f":0.775,"src":"Class test"},{"q":"With N replicas, read quorum R and write quorum W, reads see the latest write if","options":["R = 1 always","W = 0","R + W < N","R + W > N"],"answer":3,"explain":"The correct answer is D.","f":0.825,"src":"Class test"},{"q":"Raft uses randomized election timeouts to","options":["encrypt messages","avoid repeated split votes","compress logs","speed up the network"],"answer":1,"explain":"The correct answer is B.","f":0.875,"src":"Class test"},{"q":"The main benefit of consistent hashing is that when a node is added","options":["no hashing is needed","data are deleted","only a small fraction of keys move","all keys move"],"answer":2,"explain":"The correct answer is C.","f":0.925,"src":"Class test"},{"q":"An idempotent operation is one that","options":["has the same effect when repeated as when executed once","is always fast","must run exactly once","can never fail"],"answer":0,"explain":"The correct answer is A.","f":0.975,"src":"Class test"},{"q":"Strong scaling measures","options":["speedup when the problem grows with processors","speedup of a fixed-size problem as processors increase","clock frequency","memory size"],"answer":1,"explain":"The correct answer is B.","f":0.025,"src":"Final exam"},{"q":"Superlinear speedup can occur because","options":["the serial fraction is negative","it cannot occur","Amdahl's law is wrong","more caches in total can make the data fit, reducing memory accesses"],"answer":3,"explain":"The correct answer is D.","f":0.075,"src":"Final exam"},{"q":"The span of a computation DAG is","options":["the number of threads","the length of the longest chain of dependent operations","the total number of operations","the memory footprint"],"answer":1,"explain":"The correct answer is B.","f":0.125,"src":"Final exam"},{"q":"A kernel with arithmetic intensity below the roofline ridge point is","options":["compute bound","faster on more cores always","network bound","memory bound"],"answer":3,"explain":"The correct answer is D.","f":0.175,"src":"Final exam"},{"q":"An atomic fetch-and-add on a shared counter","options":["needs a barrier","completes as one indivisible operation","disables caches","is slower than no synchronization and always loses updates"],"answer":1,"explain":"The correct answer is B.","f":0.225,"src":"Final exam"},{"q":"A condition variable should be waited on inside a loop because","options":["it saves memory","wake-ups may be spurious or the condition may change before the thread runs","the loop speeds up the wait","the loop is required by the CPU"],"answer":1,"explain":"The correct answer is B.","f":0.275,"src":"Final exam"},{"q":"The ABA problem concerns","options":["clock drift","a compare-and-swap succeeding although the value changed from A to B and back to A","cache coloring","MPI collectives"],"answer":1,"explain":"The correct answer is B.","f":0.325,"src":"Final exam"},{"q":"In OpenMP, schedule(dynamic, 8) is best suited to loops whose iterations","options":["contain barriers","are empty","have irregular, unpredictable costs","are all identical"],"answer":2,"explain":"The correct answer is C.","f":0.375,"src":"Final exam"},{"q":"In a halo exchange for a grid code, the amount of communication per process grows with the","options":["volume of the subdomain","number of time steps only","program size","boundary (surface) of the subdomain"],"answer":3,"explain":"The correct answer is D.","f":0.425,"src":"Final exam"},{"q":"Two ranks both call blocking MPI_Send to each other before any receive; with large messages this may","options":["double the bandwidth","deadlock","always succeed","trigger a barrier"],"answer":1,"explain":"The correct answer is B.","f":0.475,"src":"Final exam"},{"q":"In CUDA, divergence within a warp means","options":["memory is freed","the kernel crashes","warps are split across SMs","the warp executes both branch paths serially, reducing efficiency"],"answer":3,"explain":"The correct answer is D.","f":0.525,"src":"Final exam"},{"q":"Occupancy is limited by","options":["the clock only","the compiler's name","registers per thread, shared memory per block and threads per SM","the disk size"],"answer":2,"explain":"The correct answer is C.","f":0.575,"src":"Final exam"},{"q":"FLP impossibility states that in a fully asynchronous system","options":["all clocks agree","consensus is impossible to guarantee with even one crash failure","consensus needs 3f + 1 nodes","messages are never lost"],"answer":1,"explain":"The correct answer is B.","f":0.625,"src":"Final exam"},{"q":"Byzantine fault tolerance of f faulty replicas requires at least","options":["f² replicas","f + 1 replicas","2f replicas","3f + 1 replicas"],"answer":3,"explain":"The correct answer is D.","f":0.675,"src":"Final exam"},{"q":"Two-phase commit blocks when","options":["the coordinator fails after participants have voted yes and before the decision is delivered","all vote no","a participant votes yes","the network is fast"],"answer":0,"explain":"The correct answer is A.","f":0.725,"src":"Final exam"},{"q":"With hash(key) mod N partitioning, changing N moves","options":["no keys","only one key","a fixed 1/N of keys","almost all keys"],"answer":3,"explain":"The correct answer is D.","f":0.775,"src":"Final exam"},{"q":"A Bloom filter can produce","options":["neither","both false positives and false negatives","false negatives only","false positives but no false negatives"],"answer":3,"explain":"The correct answer is D.","f":0.825,"src":"Final exam"},{"q":"Delivery semantics that require idempotent handlers are","options":["at-most-once","never","exactly-once without retries","at-least-once"],"answer":3,"explain":"The correct answer is D.","f":0.875,"src":"Final exam"},{"q":"According to the tail-at-scale argument, a request that fans out to many servers","options":["is as fast as the fastest server","is as slow as its slowest response, so rare slow responses become common","sees the average latency","is unaffected by slow nodes"],"answer":1,"explain":"The correct answer is B.","f":0.925,"src":"Final exam"},{"q":"Eventual consistency means that","options":["reads are always the latest","if no new updates occur, all replicas eventually converge to the same value","replicas never diverge","writes are never lost"],"answer":1,"explain":"The correct answer is B.","f":0.975,"src":"Final exam"}]});
